  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 88f6fa2b89436164b8e97b17d82dc9a2, type: 3}
  m_Name: 2 - Function
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: UtilityScriptIWrite::HintSO
  hintId: tutorial_functions
  isUnlockedByDefault: 0
  ignoreLock: 1
  title: Functions
  image: {fileID: 0}
  description: 'A function is a block of code that performs a specific task when
    executed.


    Functions allow programs to reuse behavior without rewriting
    the same instructions multiple times. Some functions are already pre-programmed
    and can be used directly in your code. For example: <font="JetBrainsMono SDF"><color=#F5F5AB>print</color><color=#D4D4D4></color></font>
    and <font="JetBrainsMono SDF"><color=#F5F5AB>range</color><color=#D4D4D4></color></font>


    A
    function is executed by writing its name followed by <font="JetBrainsMono SDF"><color=#D4D4D4>()</color></font>.

    Some
    functions require additional information called <color=yellow>parameters</color>.
    Parameters are written inside the parentheses and are used to control how the
    function behaves.


    Example:

    <indent=1em><font="JetBrainsMono SDF"><color=#F5F5AB>print</color>(<color=#CE9178>"Hello"</color>)</font></indent>

    In
    this example: <font="JetBrainsMono SDF"><color=#F5F5AB>print</color></font> is
    the function name and <font="JetBrainsMono SDF"><color=#CE9178>"Hello"</color></font>
    is the parameter

    Different functions may require different types of parameters
    such as numbers or text depending on their purpose.'
  isImageBig: 0
